Viability-Constrained, Reachability-Weighted Bottleneck Optimization for Human-Service Trajectory Navigation Under Partial Observability.

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta.

Abstract

The global response to homelessness is fragmented across jurisdictional, organizational, and disciplinary silos. A person experiencing homelessness must navigate a cognitive queue: locate an agency, travel to it, wait, narrate their history, be redirected elsewhere, and repeat. The bottleneck is not the absence of resources but the absence of a coordinating layer that transforms fragmented services into viable trajectories. This paper proposes a mathematical framework for AI-assisted human trajectory recovery that operates on top of existing homeless-services infrastructure rather than replacing it. The central formal contribution is the joint application of reachability and viability theory to the problem of sequencing interventions for individuals experiencing homelessness or at imminent risk thereof.

We distinguish two complementary objectives that are frequently conflated in the literature. Reachability asks whether the individual attains a stable state within a finite horizon; viability asks whether the trajectory remains within an acceptable set throughout. We define a finite-horizon reachability measure R_T(b) and a viability measure W_T(b) over a discretized, factored state space of 5{,}184 states, replace the intractable infinite-horizon viability kernel with a computable finite-horizon quantity, and derive a bottleneck identification procedure from a context-parameterized prerequisite directed acyclic graph. We characterize the computational complexity of particle-based bottleneck evaluation, present sufficient conditions for an optimal stopping policy for continuous support, and define a normalized reachability-weighted intervention efficiency. We specify an architectural design that integrates with — rather than duplicates — Homeless Management Information Systems (HMIS), Coordinated Entry, the Open Referral Human Services Data Specification (HSDS), and existing closed-loop referral networks. We formalize ethical constraints as hard architectural bounds: human authorization of sensitive decisions, user veto as easy as consent with differentiated data-retention semantics, plain-language explainability with contestability, cryptographic access-control enforcement of risk-score non-sharing, and structural accountability through disaggregated outcome reporting. We address privacy through federated learning with a formal privacy accountant, secure aggregation, and identity/case-file separation. We propose a phased evaluation design with falsifiable empirical tests and pre-registered thresholds, and we frame the framework as a computational theory of human-service trajectory navigation applicable beyond homelessness.

Keywords: viability theory, reachability optimization, bottleneck identification, homeless services, partially observable Markov decision processes, federated learning, differential privacy, algorithmic accountability

