2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 State Space and Partial Observability

Let \mathcal{X} = \prod_{i=1}^{9} \mathcal{X}_i be a factored state space with |\mathcal{X}| = 5{,}184. The state at time t is x(t) \in \mathcal{X}. Observations y_t arrive via SMS, voice, caseworker notes, or HMIS records. The belief state is

b_t(x_t) = P(x_t \mid y_{0:t}) \propto P(y_t \mid x_t) \sum_{x_{t-1}} P(x_t \mid x_{t-1}, a_{t-1}) b_{t-1}(x_{t-1}).

Belief-state filtering may be tractable under sparse or factored models. Exact POMDP optimization is not assumed tractable; we adopt particle-based approximation for the sequential decision problem.

2.2 Reachability and Viability

Definition 1 (Reachability Measure). Let \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} \subset \mathcal{X} be the target set. For a policy \pi mapping beliefs to interventions, the finite-horizon reachability measure is

R_T(b; \pi) = P\left(\tau_{\mathcal{S}} \leq T \;\middle|\; b_0 = b, \pi\right),

where

\tau_{\mathcal{S}} = \inf\{t \geq 0 : x_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}\}.

Definition 2 (Viability Measure). Let \mathcal{K} \subset \mathcal{X} be an acceptable set. The finite-horizon viability measure is

W_T(b; \pi) = P\left(x_t \in \mathcal{K},\ \forall t \leq T \;\middle|\; b_0 = b, \pi\right).

Definition 3 (Joint Objective). For weights \lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3 \geq 0, define

J_T(b; \pi) = R_T(b; \pi) + \lambda_3 W_T(b; \pi) - \lambda_1 C(\pi) - \lambda_2 \mathrm{Risk}(\pi).

The reachability term rewards attaining stability; the viability term rewards sustaining it; the cost and risk terms penalize interventions that are expensive or burdensome.

2.3 Recursive Computation with Absorbing Terminal State

To avoid double-counting the probability of already being in the target set, we introduce an absorbing terminal state x_\dagger and augment the state space:

\tilde{\mathcal{X}} = \mathcal{X} \cup \{x_\dagger\},

with transition kernel

P(x_\dagger \mid x, a) = 1 \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}},\ \forall a \in \mathcal{A}.

Proposition 1 (Recursive Computation of R_T). The reachability measure satisfies the backward recursion

R_{t+1}(b) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(b)} \sum_{y} P(y \mid b, a)\, R_t(\tau(b, a, y)),

with terminal condition

R_0(b) = \sum_{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}} b(x) + b(x_\dagger),

and R_0(b) = 1 whenever b assigns all mass to \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} \cup \{x_\dagger\}.

Proof. Standard backward induction on the belief simplex with the absorbing terminal state ensuring that once the target is reached, the event \{\tau_{\mathcal{S}} \leq T\} is satisfied for all subsequent horizons. \square

Proposition 2 (Piecewise-Linear Convexity). The value function R_t(b) is piecewise-linear and convex (PWLC) in b.

Proof. Base case: R_0(b) is linear in b. Inductive step: the belief update \tau(b, a, y) is a ratio of linear functions in b; the product P(y \mid b, a) R_t(\tau(b, a, y)) remains linear in b when R_t is PWLC, due to cancellation of the normalization term. Summation over y and maximization over a preserve PWLC. \square

The number of \alpha-vectors in the PWLC representation grows exponentially with the horizon, which is why exact solution is intractable for T = 180 and particle-based approximation is required.

2.4 Context-Parameterized Prerequisite Graph

Definition 4 (Prerequisite Graph). Let \mathcal{D} = \{H, L, F, D, E, S, R, C, I\} be the set of dimensions. For a jurisdiction j and context c, the prerequisite graph is

G_P^{(j,c)} = (\mathcal{D}, \mathcal{E}_P^{(j,c)}),

where \mathcal{E}_P^{(j,c)} encodes domain-validated prerequisite relations under jurisdiction j and context c. For example, L \to D (identity unlocks bank access), D \to E (bank access unlocks formal employment), E \to H (income unlocks rental capacity). These are not universal causal laws; different jurisdictions instantiate different graphs. All valid instances belong to the family \mathfrak{G}_{\text{HLN}}.

Definition 5 (Probabilistic Prerequisite Constraint). For a prerequisite edge (j, i) \in \mathcal{E}_P^{(j,c)} with threshold \theta_j and required confidence q_j \in (0,1], the effective action space at belief b is

\mathcal{A}(b) = \left\{ a \in \mathcal{A} : \mathrm{target}(a) = i \implies P_b(x_j \geq \theta_j) \geq q_j \ \forall (j,i) \in \mathcal{E}_P^{(j,c)} \right\}.

This formulation uses probabilistic satisfaction rather than expectation, avoiding a common error: two distributions with equal mean may have very different mass above threshold.

Lemma 1 (Monotonicity under Stochastic Dominance). If b' stochastically dominates b in the sense that P_{b'}(x_j \geq \theta_j) \geq P_b(x_j \geq \theta_j) for all j, then \mathcal{A}(b) \subseteq \mathcal{A}(b').

Proof. Immediate from Definition 5. \square

2.5 Bottleneck Optimization

Definition 6 (Reachability Gain). For intervention a and belief b:

\Delta V(a; b) = R_T(b; a) - R_T(b).

This is the Human Trajectory Reachability Gain (HTRG).

Definition 7 (Normalized Cost). Let C(a) denote financial cost, R(a) denote probabilistic risk, and B(a) denote user burden. The normalized cost is

\tilde{C}(a) = w_C C(a) + w_R R(a) + w_B B(a),

with weights w_C, w_R, w_B \geq 0 calibrated by policy.

Definition 8 (Reachability-Weighted Intervention Efficiency). For \varepsilon > 0:

\mathrm{RWIE}(a; b) = \frac{\Delta V(a; b)}{\tilde{C}(a) + \varepsilon}.

Definition 9 (Bottleneck). The bottleneck intervention is

a^*(b) = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(b)} \mathrm{RWIE}(a; b).

The term B(a) is essential: two interventions with equal financial cost may impose very different human burdens.

2.6 Particle-Based Bottleneck Evaluation

Proposition 3 (Computational Complexity). Under a factored state space and particle-based belief representation with K particles, evaluation of R_T(b; a) for a single intervention a has complexity

O\left(K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}\right),

where C_{\text{transition}} is the cost of propagating one particle through the transition model. The full bottleneck search over \mathcal{A}(b) has complexity

O\left(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}\right).

For a factored model with sparse dependencies, C_{\text{transition}} \ll |\mathcal{X}|. With |\mathcal{A}| \leq 20, K = 10^3, T = 180, and C_{\text{transition}} = O(1), the computation is feasible on commodity hardware within seconds.

Proof. For each candidate a, we run K particles forward for T steps; each step costs O(K \cdot C_{\text{transition}}). With |\mathcal{A}| candidates, total complexity follows. \square

2.7 Sequential Intervention Optimization

The real problem is often a sequence a_1 \to a_2 \to \cdots \to a_n. The true objective is

\pi^* = \arg\max_{\pi} \mathbb{E}\left[ R_T(b; \pi) + \lambda_3 W_T(b; \pi) - \lambda_1 C(\pi) - \lambda_2 \mathrm{Risk}(\pi) \right].

The bottleneck at each step is the intervention that unlocks the best future trajectory, not merely the intervention with the highest local gain.

2.8 Optimal Stopping for Continuous Support

Definition 10 (Stall Event). A stall event occurs at time t if

R_{T-t}(b_t) < \epsilon.

Definition 11 (Stopping Problem). At each time t, the system chooses between continuing (no intervention) and intervening (re-assessment, re-recommendation). Let c_{\text{intervene}} be the cost of intervention and c_{\text{stall}} the cost of failing to intervene when a stall is occurring.

Proposition 4 (Threshold Structure under Sufficient Conditions). Under the assumptions of monotone likelihood ratio ordering, convex costs, stationary dynamics, and a single-threshold continuation region, the optimal policy has the form

\pi^*(b_t) = \begin{cases} \text{intervene} & \text{if } P(\text{stall} \mid b_t) \geq p^* \\ \text{continue} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

with

p^* = \frac{c_{\text{intervene}}}{c_{\text{stall}} + c_{\text{intervene}}}.

Proof sketch. Under the stated assumptions, the value function of the stopping problem is monotone in the posterior probability of stall, and the continuation region is convex. Standard results for optimal stopping under partial observations yield threshold policies. \square

2.9 Three-Way Output: Recommend, Defer, Abstain

The system may output one of three actions:

· Recommend if \mathrm{LCB}[\Delta V(a) - \lambda \tilde{C}(a)] \geq \delta, where LCB denotes the lower confidence bound.
· Defer if the point estimate is promising but the confidence interval is wide relative to \delta.
· Abstain if no intervention exceeds \delta.

This three-way output makes uncertainty explicit and supports meaningful human oversight.

2.10 Full Model

\mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{Y}, P, O, G_P^{(j,c)}, C, \mathcal{V}, T),

with \mathcal{X} the state space, \mathcal{A} the intervention space, \mathcal{Y} the observation space, P the transition model, O the observation model, G_P^{(j,c)} the context-parameterized prerequisite graph, C the normalized cost, \mathcal{V} the viability set, and T the planning horizon.

Success is defined as

\text{Success} = P\left( x_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{autonomy}} \text{ and } \mathrm{SupportDemand}_t \to 0 \right).

The system succeeds when the individual no longer needs it.

