5. Discussion

5.1 Limitations

The framework assumes that the state space and prerequisite graph are adequately specified. In practice, these require iterative refinement with domain experts. The transition model estimation is constrained by HMIS data quality; exit data completeness for Emergency Shelter and Street Outreach are particularly problematic. Federated learning mitigates this by pooling data across CoCs, but the underlying incompleteness introduces bias that must be modeled explicitly, for example through multiple imputation and inverse probability weighting.

The optimal stopping policy is presented under sufficient assumptions; the closed-form threshold p^* may not hold in more general settings. The cryptographic access-control policy for C4 is not an absolute guarantee against exfiltration under all threat models; it is a construction that enforces the policy under stated assumptions.

The framework is parameterized by jurisdiction and context. We do not claim universal applicability of any particular prerequisite graph; we claim a general family \mathfrak{G}_{\text{HLN}} within which jurisdiction-specific instances are validated.

5.2 Failure Modes

Potential failure modes include inaccurate belief states due to poor observations; incorrect prerequisite graphs leading to suboptimal or harmful recommendations; privacy budget exhaustion leading to degraded utility; algorithmic bias leading to disparate impact; and user distrust leading to low adoption. Each of these is addressed by the architectural constraints and the evaluation design.

5.3 Generalization Beyond Homelessness

The framework is a computational theory of human-service trajectory navigation, instantiated here for homelessness. The same architecture applies to housing-loss prevention, hospital discharge, prison reentry, refugee resettlement, disaster response, extreme poverty, rural isolation, and youth aging out of care. The paper remains focused on homelessness to preserve a falsifiable scientific question.

---

6. Conclusion

We have presented a viability-constrained, reachability-weighted bottleneck optimization framework for human-service trajectory navigation under partial observability. The framework distinguishes reachability from viability, defines a context-parameterized prerequisite graph, derives a bottleneck identification procedure with normalized cost and risk, and specifies an architecture that integrates with existing infrastructure. Ethical constraints are formalized as hard architectural bounds. Privacy is addressed through federated learning with a formal privacy accountant, secure aggregation, and identity/case-file separation. A phased evaluation design with falsifiable tests and pre-registered thresholds is proposed. The system succeeds when the individual no longer needs it.

