A.2 Proof of Lemma 1 (Probabilistic Prerequisite Constraint and Monotonicity)

Statement. For a prerequisite edge (j,i) \in \mathcal{E}_P^{(j,c)} with threshold \theta_j and required confidence q_j \in (0,1], the effective action space at belief b is

\mathcal{A}(b) = \left\{ a \in \mathcal{A} : \mathrm{target}(a) = i \implies P_b(x_j \geq \theta_j) \geq q_j \ \forall (j,i) \in \mathcal{E}_P^{(j,c)} \right\}.

Moreover, if b' stochastically dominates b in the sense that P_{b'}(x_j \geq \theta_j) \geq P_b(x_j \geq \theta_j) for all j, then \mathcal{A}(b) \subseteq \mathcal{A}(b').

Proof. The first part is a definition, not a claim to prove. For the second part, let a \in \mathcal{A}(b). By definition, for every prerequisite (j,i) with \mathrm{target}(a)=i, we have P_b(x_j \geq \theta_j) \geq q_j. By hypothesis, P_{b'}(x_j \geq \theta_j) \geq P_b(x_j \geq \theta_j) \geq q_j. Therefore a \in \mathcal{A}(b'). Since a was arbitrary, \mathcal{A}(b) \subseteq \mathcal{A}(b'). \square

Remark A.2.1. The use of P_b(x_j \geq \theta_j) rather than \mathbb{E}_b[x_j] is essential. Two distributions with identical means may have very different mass above threshold. Formulating prerequisites in terms of expectation can therefore produce incorrect action admissibility. The probabilistic formulation avoids this and admits a clean monotonicity result under stochastic dominance.

Remark A.2.2. If one instead assumes a stronger ordering—first-order stochastic dominance on each dimension j—then P_{b'}(x_j \geq \theta_j) \geq P_b(x_j \geq \theta_j) holds for all thresholds \theta_j, and the inclusion \mathcal{A}(b) \subseteq \mathcal{A}(b') is guaranteed.

---

A.3 Proof of Proposition 1 (Recursive Computation of R_T)

Statement. With the absorbing terminal state x_\dagger satisfying P(x_\dagger \mid x, a) = 1 for all x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} and all a, the reachability measure satisfies

R_{t+1}(b) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(b)} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} P(y \mid b, a)\, R_t(\tau(b,a,y)),

with terminal condition

R_0(b) = \sum_{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}} b(x) + b(x_\dagger).

Proof. We proceed by induction on t.

Base case. By definition, R_0(b) = P(\tau_{\mathcal{S}} \leq 0 \mid b_0 = b) = P(x_0 \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} \mid b_0 = b). Since x_0 \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} iff the state is either in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} or in the absorbing terminal x_\dagger (which by construction represents having already succeeded), we have

R_0(b) = \sum_{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}} b(x) + b(x_\dagger).

Inductive step. Assume R_t(b) = P(\tau_{\mathcal{S}} \leq t \mid b_0 = b, \pi^*) for some optimal \pi^*. For the horizon t+1, fix the first action a \in \mathcal{A}(b). By the law of total probability, conditioning on the first observation y,

P(\tau_{\mathcal{S}} \leq t+1 \mid b_0 = b, a_0 = a) = \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} P(y \mid b, a)\, P(\tau_{\mathcal{S}} \leq t \mid b_1 = \tau(b,a,y), a_0 = a).

The second factor is the reachability probability under the horizon-t problem starting from the updated belief \tau(b,a,y). By the inductive hypothesis, this equals R_t(\tau(b,a,y)), assuming the continuation policy from that point is optimal.

Maximizing over a \in \mathcal{A}(b),

R_{t+1}(b) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(b)} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} P(y \mid b, a)\, R_t(\tau(b,a,y)).

This is the stated recursion. \square

Remark A.3.1. The absorbing terminal state prevents double-counting. Without it, the recursion would include a term p_{\text{stable}}(b) at every time step, overcounting the probability of already having reached \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}. Introducing x_\dagger allows the reward to be collected exactly once at the moment of arrival.

---

A.4 Proof of Proposition 2 (PWLC of R_t)

Statement. The value function R_t(b) is piecewise-linear and convex (PWLC) in b.

Proof. We proceed by induction on t.

Base case. R_0(b) = \sum_{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}} b(x) + b(x_\dagger) = \alpha_0 \cdot b for the vector \alpha_0 with \alpha_0(x) = \mathbf{1}\{x \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}\} + \mathbf{1}\{x = x_\dagger\}. This is linear in b, hence PWLC.

Inductive step. Assume

R_t(b) = \max_{\alpha \in \Gamma_t} \sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \alpha(x) b(x) = \max_{\alpha \in \Gamma_t} (\alpha \cdot b)

for some finite set of \alpha-vectors \Gamma_t.

Fix a \in \mathcal{A}(b) and y \in \mathcal{Y}. Define

N_y(b)(x') = O(y\mid x')\sum_x P(x'\mid x,a)b(x), \qquad D_y(b) = \sum_{x''} N_y(b)(x'').

Both N_y(b) and D_y(b) are linear in b, and D_y(b) = P(y\mid b,a) > 0 whenever y is observable. The updated belief is \tau(b,a,y) = N_y(b)/D_y(b). Therefore

R_t(\tau(b,a,y)) = \max_{\alpha \in \Gamma_t} \sum_{x'} \alpha(x') \frac{N_y(b)(x')}{D_y(b)} = \frac{1}{D_y(b)} \max_{\alpha \in \Gamma_t} L_{y,\alpha}(b),

where L_{y,\alpha}(b) = \sum_{x'} \alpha(x') N_y(b)(x') is linear in b. Multiplying by P(y\mid b,a) = D_y(b):

P(y\mid b,a)\, R_t(\tau(b,a,y)) = D_y(b) \cdot \frac{1}{D_y(b)} \max_{\alpha \in \Gamma_t} L_{y,\alpha}(b) = \max_{\alpha \in \Gamma_t} L_{y,\alpha}(b).

The factor D_y(b) > 0 is a positive scalar independent of \alpha, so it commutes with the maximum. The resulting expression is a maximum of linear functions in b, hence PWLC.

Summing over y \in \mathcal{Y}:

\sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} P(y\mid b,a) R_t(\tau(b,a,y)) = \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} \max_{\alpha \in \Gamma_t} L_{y,\alpha}(b) = \max_{(\alpha_y)_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} \in \Gamma_t^{|\mathcal{Y}|}} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} L_{y,\alpha_y}(b).

The final expression is a maximum over the product set \Gamma_t^{|\mathcal{Y}|} of linear functions in b, hence PWLC.

Finally, maximizing over a \in \mathcal{A}(b):

R_{t+1}(b) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(b)} \left[ \text{PWLC function of } b \right].

The pointwise maximum of convex functions is convex, and the pointwise maximum of piecewise-linear functions is piecewise-linear. Therefore R_{t+1}(b) is PWLC. \square

Corollary A.4.1. The number of \alpha-vectors in the exact representation grows as |\Gamma_{t+1}| \in O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot |\Gamma_t|^{|\mathcal{Y}|}). For |\mathcal{Y}| \geq 2 and T = 180, exact solution is infeasible.

Corollary A.4.2. The reachability objective is compatible with particle-based approximation: for any \delta > 0 and any finite K, there exists K_0(\delta) such that for K \geq K_0, the particle-based estimate \hat{R}_T(b) satisfies |\hat{R}_T(b) - R_T(b)| \leq \delta with high probability. This is a standard consequence of the law of large numbers applied to the particle filter.

---

A.5 Proof of Proposition 3 (Complexity of Particle-Based Bottleneck Evaluation)

Statement. Under a factored state space and particle-based belief representation with K particles, evaluating R_T(b;a) for a single intervention a has complexity

O\left(K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}\right),

and the full bottleneck search over \mathcal{A}(b) has complexity

O\left(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}\right),

where C_{\text{transition}} is the cost of propagating one particle through the transition kernel.

Proof. Fix a \in \mathcal{A}(b). The particle filter proceeds as follows.

1. Initialization. Sample K particles \{x^{(k)}_0\}_{k=1}^{K} from b_0. Cost: O(K).
2. Propagation. For each time step t = 0, 1, \ldots, T-1:
   · For each particle k, sample x^{(k)}_{t+1} \sim P(\cdot \mid x^{(k)}_t, a). Under the factored model with sparse dependencies, this costs C_{\text{transition}} per particle.
   · For each particle k, compute the observation likelihood O(y_t \mid x^{(k)}_{t+1}) and update weights. Cost: O(K).
   · Resample if effective sample size falls below threshold. Cost: O(K).
   Total per step: O(K \cdot C_{\text{transition}}).
3. Reachability accumulation. At each step, accumulate the mass of particles in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} \cup \{x_\dagger\}. Cost: O(K) per step.

Total for the simulation: O(K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}).

For the full bottleneck search, we evaluate R_T(b;a) for each a \in \mathcal{A}(b). Since |\mathcal{A}(b)| \leq |\mathcal{A}|, the total cost is

O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}).

\square

Remark A.5.1. For our factored model, C_{\text{transition}} is bounded as follows. The transition factorizes as

P(x'\mid x, a) = \prod_{i=1}^{9} P(x'_i \mid \mathrm{pa}(x'_i), a),

where \mathrm{pa}(x'_i) \subset \mathcal{X} is the set of parents of x'_i in the DAG. Sampling each factor costs O(|\mathcal{X}_i|) if we sample from the categorical distribution directly. Hence

C_{\text{transition}} = O\left(\sum_{i=1}^{9} |\mathcal{X}_i|\right) = O(24),

which is O(1) in the problem parameters. The overall complexity is therefore O(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T), matching the simpler expression in the main text.

---

A.6 Proof of Proposition 4 (Threshold Structure of Optimal Stopping)

Statement. Under assumptions (A1)–(A4) below, the optimal stopping policy for the Continuous Support Layer has the threshold form

\pi^*(b_t) = \begin{cases} \text{intervene} & \text{if } P(\text{stall} \mid b_t) \geq p^* \\ \text{continue} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

with p^* = c_{\text{intervene}} / c_{\text{stall}} in the myopic (one-step) case, and a possibly different value in the multi-step case that remains monotone in c_{\text{intervene}} and c_{\text{stall}}.

Assumptions.

· (A1) The belief process (b_t)_{t \geq 0} is a controlled Markov chain under the belief update operator \tau.
· (A2) The observation likelihood satisfies the monotone likelihood ratio property (MLRP) in the sense that higher b_t in the stochastic order yields higher P(\text{stall} \mid b_t).
· (A3) The costs c_{\text{intervene}} and c_{\text{stall}} are stationary (time-invariant).
· (A4) The value function of the stopping problem is monotone in P(\text{stall} \mid b_t) and the continuation region is convex.

Proof (myopic case). Consider the one-step problem. At time t, the decision-maker chooses u_t \in \{\text{continue}, \text{intervene}\}. Under the myopic rule, the decision at time t is made as if this were the last decision opportunity. Then:

· If u_t = \text{intervene}: the cost is c_{\text{intervene}} with certainty.
· If u_t = \text{continue}: with probability P(\text{stall} \mid b_t), the cost is c_{\text{stall}}; otherwise, the cost is zero.

Intervention is optimal iff the expected cost of intervening does not exceed the expected cost of continuing:

c_{\text{intervene}} \leq c_{\text{stall}} \cdot P(\text{stall} \mid b_t).

Rearranging:

P(\text{stall} \mid b_t) \geq \frac{c_{\text{intervene}}}{c_{\text{stall}}}.

Setting p^* = c_{\text{intervene}} / c_{\text{stall}} yields the threshold rule. \square

Proof (multi-step case, sketch). Let J_t(p_t) denote the value function of the stopping problem, where p_t = P(\text{stall} \mid b_t). Under (A1)–(A4):

· J_t is monotone non-decreasing in p_t.
· J_t is convex in p_t (standard for stopping problems).
· The continuation value V^{\text{cont}}_t(p_t) = \mathbb{E}[J_{t+1}(p_{t+1}) \mid p_t, \text{continue}] is also monotone and convex.

The intervention value is V^{\text{int}}_t = -c_{\text{intervene}} + \mathbb{E}[J_{t+1}(p_{t+1}) \mid p_t, \text{intervene}]. The optimal policy is to intervene iff

V^{\text{int}}_t \geq V^{\text{cont}}_t.

Because both value functions are monotone in p_t and their difference is monotone (by (A2)), the set of p_t for which intervention is optimal is an interval of the form [p^*_t, 1]. Hence the optimal policy has a threshold structure with time-varying threshold p^*_t.

Monotonicity of p^*_t in c_{\text{intervene}} and c_{\text{stall}} follows from the envelope theorem applied to the value function. \square

Remark A.6.1. The myopic threshold p^* = c_{\text{intervene}}/c_{\text{stall}} is a lower bound on the true multi-step threshold when continuing has option value (i.e., when waiting preserves the possibility of intervening later). In that case the true threshold is higher. The myopic formula is used as a heuristic initialization, and the exact threshold is computed numerically under (A1)–(A4).

Remark A.6.2. When intervention does not guarantee stall avoidance (stochastic intervention), the threshold becomes p^* = c_{\text{intervene}} / (c_{\text{stall}} \cdot \rho), where \rho \in (0,1] is the intervention efficacy. This reduces to the deterministic case when \rho = 1.

---

A.7 Recursive Computation of W_T (Viability)

We define viability via an absorbing failure state x_\ddagger. Augment the state space to \tilde{\mathcal{X}} = \mathcal{X} \cup \{x_\ddagger\} and define the modified transition kernel

P^{\mathcal{K}}(x'\mid x,a) =
\begin{cases}
P(x'\mid x,a) & \text{if } x \in \mathcal{K},\ x' \in \mathcal{K}, \\
1 - \sum_{x'' \in \mathcal{K}} P(x''\mid x,a) & \text{if } x \in \mathcal{K},\ x' = x_\ddagger, \\
0 & \text{if } x \in \mathcal{K},\ x' \notin \mathcal{K} \cup \{x_\ddagger\}, \\
0 & \text{if } x = x_\ddagger,\ x' \neq x_\ddagger, \\
1 & \text{if } x = x_\ddagger,\ x' = x_\ddagger.
\end{cases}

Under P^{\mathcal{K}}, the failure state x_\ddagger is absorbing. Then

W_T(b) = P\left(x_t \in \mathcal{K}\ \forall t \leq T \mid b_0 = b\right) = P\left(x_T \neq x_\ddagger \mid b_0 = b\right) \text{ under } P^{\mathcal{K}}.

Proposition A.7.1 (Recursive Computation of W_T). The viability measure satisfies

W_{t+1}(b) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}(b)} \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} P^{\mathcal{K}}(y\mid b, a)\, W_t(\tau^{\mathcal{K}}(b,a,y)),

with terminal condition

W_0(b) = 1 - b(x_\ddagger).

Proof. The proof is structurally identical to Proposition 1 but with the failure state replacing the success state. Base case:

W_0(b) = P(x_0 \in \mathcal{K} \mid b_0 = b) = 1 - b(x_\ddagger).

Inductive step follows from the law of total probability and the Markov property of the killed process under P^{\mathcal{K}}. \square

Corollary A.7.2. W_T(b) is PWLC in b, by the same argument as Proposition 2 applied to P^{\mathcal{K}} and \tau^{\mathcal{K}}.

Corollary A.7.3. W_T(b) \leq R_T(b) whenever \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} \subseteq \mathcal{K}, since the event \{x_t \in \mathcal{K}\ \forall t \leq T\} implies \{x_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}}\ \text{for some } t \leq T\} when \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} \subseteq \mathcal{K} and the process reaches \mathcal{S}_{\text{stable}} before exiting \mathcal{K}.

---

A.8 Well-Posedness of the Joint Objective

Proposition A.8.1 (Boundedness). For any b \in \Delta(\mathcal{X}) and any policy \pi,

0 \leq R_T(b;\pi) \leq 1, \qquad 0 \leq W_T(b;\pi) \leq 1,

and

-\lambda_1 C_{\max} - \lambda_2 R_{\max} \leq J_T(b;\pi) \leq 1 + \lambda_3.

Proof. Immediate from the fact that R_T, W_T are probabilities and C, R are bounded by their maxima over the finite action set. \square

Proposition A.8.2 (Monotonicity in weights). The optimal policy \pi^* satisfies:

· \partial \pi^* / \partial \lambda_1 \leq 0: as the weight on cost increases, the optimal policy shifts toward lower-cost interventions.
· \partial \pi^* / \partial \lambda_2 \leq 0: as the weight on risk increases, the optimal policy shifts toward lower-risk interventions.
· \partial \pi^* / \partial \lambda_3 \geq 0: as the weight on viability increases, the optimal policy shifts toward interventions that preserve acceptable states.

Proof. Standard comparative statics for the argmax of a differentiable objective with monotone cross-partials. The claims hold pointwise for each b where the argmax is unique. \square

---

A.9 Abstention and Deferral as Decision-Theoretic Constructs

Let \hat{\Delta V}(a;b) denote the point estimate of the reachability gain, and let [L(a), U(a)] denote a (1-\alpha)-confidence interval.

Define the lower confidence bound of the net utility:

\mathrm{LCB}(a;b) = L(a) - \lambda \tilde{C}(a).

Define the upper confidence bound:

\mathrm{UCB}(a;b) = U(a) - \lambda \tilde{C}(a).

Definition A.9.1 (Recommend / Defer / Abstain). For a threshold \delta > 0:

· Recommend a if \mathrm{LCB}(a;b) \geq \delta.
· Defer a if \mathrm{LCB}(a;b) < \delta but \mathrm{UCB}(a;b) \geq \delta.
· Abstain if \mathrm{UCB}(a;b) < \delta for all a \in \mathcal{A}(b).

Proposition A.9.2 (Three-Way Partition). The three categories partition the action space \mathcal{A}(b) for any \delta and any confidence level.

Proof. For each a, exactly one of \mathrm{LCB}(a;b) \geq \delta, or \mathrm{LCB}(a;b) < \delta \leq \mathrm{UCB}(a;b), or \mathrm{UCB}(a;b) < \delta holds, since L(a) \leq U(a) implies \mathrm{LCB}(a;b) \leq \mathrm{UCB}(a;b). \square

Proposition A.9.3 (Conservatism). The Recommend decision rule is conservative: it recommends only interventions whose worst-case (lower-bound) net utility exceeds \delta. This satisfies the "first, do no harm" principle with respect to the estimated utility.

---

A.10 Non-Identifiability and Partial Observability

Proposition A.10.1 (Non-Identifiability of the State). Let b \in \Delta(\mathcal{X}) be a belief. For any partition \mathcal{P} of \mathcal{X} into subsets on which the observation model O(y\mid x) is constant, the belief b and any belief b' obtained from b by redistributing mass within each element of \mathcal{P} produce identical predictive distributions over future observations.

Proof. For any future observation sequence y_{0:t}, the likelihood is

P(y_{0:t}\mid b) = \prod_{s=0}^{t} P(y_s \mid b_s),

where b_s depends on b through the belief update. If O(y\mid x) is constant on each element of \mathcal{P}, then P(y_s\mid b_s) depends on b_s only through its marginal over \mathcal{P}. Thus any redistribution within each element of \mathcal{P} produces identical likelihoods. \square

Corollary A.10.2. The reachability measure R_T(b;a) is invariant under redistributions of belief mass within observation-equivalent subsets of \mathcal{X}. The bottleneck ranking a^* depends on the belief only through its projection onto the quotient space \mathcal{X}/\mathcal{P}.

Remark A.10.3. This non-identifiability is a fundamental property of partially observable decision processes, not a defect of the model. It implies that two individuals with the same observation history may have different true states but must receive the same recommendation. The ethical implication is that the system must not over-claim precision in its state estimates, and must communicate uncertainty in plain language.

