Appendix B: Computational Complexity Analysis

B.1 Background: POMDP Complexity

Finding an optimal policy for a finite-horizon POMDP is PSPACE-complete in the size of the state space and horizon; for infinite horizons it is undecidable. The complexity is a function of:

· |\mathcal{S}|, number of states;
· |\mathcal{A}|, number of actions;
· |\mathcal{Z}|, number of observations;
· T, horizon.

Exact solution requires manipulation of the piecewise-linear convex value function, represented by a set of \alpha-vectors. The number of \alpha-vectors needed grows exponentially in the number of observations at each iteration:

|\Gamma_t| \in O\left(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot |\Gamma_{t-1}|^{|\mathcal{Z}|}\right).

For T = 180, exact solution is infeasible. We adopt particle-based approximation.

B.2 Factored Belief Filtering Cost

Under the factored state space and sparse transition model, the belief update operator \tau factorizes into nine marginal updates, one per dimension, each of which depends on the marginal of the parent dimensions. For a DAG with maximum in-degree k, the cost of a full belief update is

O\left(\sum_{i=1}^{9} |\mathcal{X}_i| \cdot \prod_{j \in \mathrm{pa}(i)} |\mathcal{X}_j|\right).

For our model with k \leq 3 and |\mathcal{X}_i| \leq 4, this is O(4^4 \cdot 9) = O(2304) operations per update. This is well within real-time budgets.

B.3 Particle Filter Approximation Error

The particle filter with K particles approximates the belief b_t with mean squared error O(1/K). For K = 10^3, the approximation error is approximately 0.03 in total variation distance. This is sufficient for the bottleneck ranking, which requires distinguishing interventions with effect sizes of at least 0.05 (5 percentage points in viability).

The convergence is in probability: for any \delta > 0,

P\left(\sup_{b \in \Delta(\mathcal{X})} \|\hat{b}_t - b_t\|_{TV} > \delta\right) \to 0 \quad \text{as } K \to \infty.

B.4 Bottleneck Search Cost

The bottleneck search over \mathcal{A}(b) has complexity

O\left(|\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}\right).

For our parameter regime:

· |\mathcal{A}| \leq 20;
· K = 10^3;
· T = 180;
· C_{\text{transition}} = 24.

Total:

20 \times 1000 \times 180 \times 24 = 8.64 \times 10^7 \text{ operations}.

This is feasible on commodity hardware within seconds, and can be further parallelized across actions and particles.

B.5 Sequential Intervention Cost

For a sequence of n interventions, the cost of evaluating a single sequence is O(n \cdot |\mathcal{A}| \cdot K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}). For sequences of length n \leq 5 and the same parameters, this is O(4.3 \times 10^8) operations, feasible within a minute. If the search is over all sequences in a tree of depth n and branching factor |\mathcal{A}|, the cost grows exponentially. We therefore restrict to greedy or beam-search approximations.

B.6 Optimal Stopping Cost

The optimal stopping policy requires computing W_{T-t}(b_t) at each time step. This is the same computation as the bottleneck search but with a single action (continue vs. intervene). The cost is O(K \cdot T \cdot C_{\text{transition}}) per time step, or O(K \cdot T^2 \cdot C_{\text{transition}}) over the full horizon. With T = 180:

1000 \times 180^2 \times 24 \approx 7.8 \times 10^8 \text{ operations}.

This is feasible with offline computation and online lookup.

B.7 Memory and Storage

· Belief vector (dense): 5{,}184 \times 8 bytes \approx 41 KB per individual.
· Particle representation: 10^3 \times 9 \times 1 byte \approx 9 KB per individual.
· \alpha-vectors (truncated to |Γ_t| \leq 10^4): 10^4 \times 5{,}184 \times 8 bytes \approx 415 MB. This is the dominant cost and is shared across all individuals (not per-individual).
· Transition model (factored, sparse): 9 \times 4^4 \times 4 \approx 9{,}216 parameters, negligible.

