Appendix D: Transition Model Estimation

We emphasize that no transition model has been estimated and no federated training has been performed. The material in this appendix is a proposed protocol, not a report of empirical work.

D.1 Hierarchical Bayesian Specification

For each transition x \to x' under action a, the transition probability is

P(x'\mid x, a) = \begin{cases} p_{x,a,x'} & \text{if } (x,x') \in \mathcal{T}(a), \\ 0 & \text{otherwise}, \end{cases}

where \mathcal{T}(a) is the set of transitions enabled by action a under the prerequisite graph.

The prior on p_{x,a,x'} is a Dirichlet distribution:

(p_{x,a,x'})_{x' \in \mathcal{X}} \sim \mathrm{Dirichlet}(\alpha_{x,a,\cdot}).

The hyperparameters \alpha_{x,a,x'} are elicited from domain experts via structured elicitation.

D.2 Prior Elicitation Protocol

For each enabled transition x \to x' under action a, the expert provides:

1. A best estimate \hat{p}_{x,a,x'}.
2. A 90\% credible interval [p_{\text{lo}}, p_{\text{hi}}].
3. A qualitative justification.

The Dirichlet hyperparameters are chosen to match the elicited mean and variance:

\mathbb{E}[p] = \frac{\alpha}{\alpha_0}, \qquad \mathrm{Var}[p] = \frac{\alpha(\alpha_0 - \alpha)}{\alpha_0^2(\alpha_0+1)},

where \alpha_0 = \sum_{x'} \alpha_{x,a,x'}. Solving for \alpha and \alpha_0 gives the prior.

D.3 Federated Posterior Update

Each CoC i computes a local posterior over the transition parameters using its HMIS data:

P(p_{x,a,x'} \mid \mathcal{D}_i) \propto P(\mathcal{D}_i \mid p_{x,a,x'}) P(p_{x,a,x'}).

The local posterior is perturbed with DP noise and transmitted to the secure aggregation server. The server aggregates and returns the global posterior:

P(p_{x,a,x'} \mid \mathcal{D}) \propto P(p_{x,a,x'}) \prod_{i=1}^{N} P(\mathcal{D}_i \mid p_{x,a,x'}).

D.4 Cold-Start Strategy

HMIS data are sparse and uneven. The cold-start strategy is:

1. Prior elicitation: use caseworker priors for all transitions.
2. Synthetic data augmentation: generate synthetic trajectories from the prior to initialize the particle filter.
3. Transfer learning: use transition models from similar CoCs (with similar demographics and service landscapes) as informative priors.
4. Incremental learning: update the model as data accumulate, starting with a high learning rate and decaying over time.

D.5 Missing Data and Bias Correction

HMIS data completeness varies by project type. We propose:

· Multiple imputation with chained equations (MICE): impute missing exit destinations.
· Sensitivity analysis: test the robustness of the transition model to different imputation strategies.
· Inverse probability weighting (IPW): correct for non-random missingness.

D.6 Model Validation Plan

· Posterior predictive checks: compare simulated trajectories to held-out HMIS trajectories.
· Calibration: check that predicted probabilities match observed frequencies.
· Expert review: present the learned transition probabilities to caseworkers for qualitative validation.

D.7 Expert Review and Revision Protocol

The prerequisite graph G_P^{(j,c)} is reviewed quarterly by a panel of caseworkers, service providers, and individuals with lived experience. The review process includes:

1. Presentation of the current graph.
2. Identification of missing or outdated edges.
3. Revision based on consensus.
4. Versioning and documentation of changes.

