Appendix E: Implementation Technical Specification

E.1 HLN-EL Grammar and Semantics

Definition E.1.1 (HLN Eligibility Language). HLN-EL is a first-order fragment defined by the following grammar:

```
<predicate> ::= <atom>
              | (not <predicate>)
              | (<predicate> and <predicate>)
              | (<predicate> or <predicate>)
<atom>      ::= <variable> <op> <value>
<variable>  ::= H | L | F | D | E | S | R | C | I
<op>        ::= = | != | < | > | <= | >=
<value>     ::= integer | boolean
```

Proposition E.1.2 (Decidability). Every HLN-EL expression is decidable in O(1) time against a feature vector.

Proof. By structural induction. Atoms compare a single variable against a constant, costing O(1). Boolean connectives combine subexpressions in O(1). There is no recursion or quantification, so the size of any expression is bounded by the grammar depth, which is finite and independent of the state space. \square

Proposition E.1.3 (Static Validation). Every HLN-EL expression can be statically validated against a schema specifying the domains of the variables. Ill-formed expressions are rejected at publish time.

E.2 API Specification

The HLN optimization endpoint is a read-only extension to the HSDS API:

