Appendix F: Evaluation Specifications

We emphasize that no evaluation has been conducted. All protocols below are proposed.

F.1 Test 1: Bottleneck Identification Accuracy

Protocol.

· Sample size: N = 50\text{--}100 cases.
· Expert panel: 3 blinded expert caseworkers with \geq 5 years experience.
· Procedure: each panelist independently ranks the top 3 bottlenecks for each case.

Outcomes.

· Primary: top-1 agreement between AI and panel majority.
· Secondary: Spearman rank correlation across top 3.

Thresholds.

· Top-1 agreement \geq 70\%.
· Spearman rank correlation \geq 0.6.
· False bottleneck rate < 5\%.
· Inter-rater reliability reported using Fleiss' \kappa.

F.2 Test 2: Time-to-Stabilization

Protocol.

· Design: stepped-wedge cluster randomized trial.
· Number of clusters: determined prospectively by

N_{\text{clusters}} = f(\text{effect size}, \text{ICC}, \text{power}, \alpha, \text{attrition}, \text{design}).

· Primary outcome: time from first contact to stable housing (30 consecutive days in permanent housing).
· Analysis: survival models with intention-to-treat and site-level random effects.

Threshold. 15\% reduction in time-to-stabilization, with p < 0.05.

Power Analysis (illustrative). With \text{ICC} = 0.05, 80\% power, \alpha = 0.05, and 15\% effect size, a stepped-wedge design with N_{\text{clusters}} = 8 and N_{\text{periods}} = 5 with 20 individuals per cluster-period yields an effective sample size of approximately 250. The exact number will be computed via simulation at protocol finalization.

F.3 Test 3: Trajectory Durability

Protocol.

· Sample: all individuals who stabilized in Test 2.
· Primary outcome: return to homelessness within 12 months.
· Analysis: logistic regression with site fixed effects and adjustment for baseline risk.

Threshold. 10 percentage point reduction in 12-month return rate, defined as the Minimum Important Difference (MID), with p < 0.05.

F.4 Test 4: Dignity, Consent, and Autonomy

Protocol.

· Design: mixed methods.
· Quantitative: validated scales for autonomy, trust, perceived surveillance.
· Qualitative: semi-structured interviews with 20 participants, including opt-outs.
· Instrument pre-registered.

Thresholds.

· No more than 10\% of users report feeling surveilled, judged, or coerced.
· Median autonomy subscale at or above midpoint.

F.5 Pre-Registration Requirements

· Primary and secondary outcomes.
· Analysis plan (including subgroup analyses).
· Sample size and power calculations.
· Stopping rules.
· Handling of missing data.
· Ethical approvals.

F.6 Process Metrics

Metric Target
Time from first message to first referral < 48 hours
Referral completion rate \geq 60\%
Resource data staleness Within resource-specific TTL
False bottleneck rate < 5\%
Retention through first stabilization step \geq 50\%
Cost per successful trajectory initiation < \$5{,}000

F.7 Statistical Analysis Plan

· Primary analysis: intention-to-treat.
· Secondary analysis: per-protocol.
· Subgroup analyses: pre-specified (by race, gender, disability, age).
· Missing data: multiple imputation by chained equations.
· Multiplicity: Bonferroni correction for secondary outcomes.

